+The HTTP bridge calls `/update_ttft_slo`, `/update_tbt_slo`, `/update_tpot_slo`
+with the matching JSON key in **seconds**, only when a real policy consumes it.
+Typed client functions now also call the implemented POST `/update_chunk_size`
+and `/update_microbatch_size` routes and GET `/beam_status`. Bodies contain a
+positive integer under the matching key, or `{"clear": true}`; bools, empty
+commands and value+clear combinations are rejected. Returned JSON acknowledgments
+are retained, validated and distinguished from effective state. These controls
+are real in both the artifact and target; the target changes owner phases through
+flush/retire instead of resizing the execution queue.
+`/start_profile` takes `{"output_dir": "absolute server-side directory"}`;
+`/stop_profile` takes `{}`. Empty HTTP 200 bodies are successful responses.
+HTTP/auth failures abort the workflow, rather than being parsed as metrics.
+
+`run` accepts `--chunk-size N` / `--clear-chunk-size` and
+`--microbatch-size M` / `--clear-microbatch-size` / `--microbatch-pattern M...`.
+Microbatch commands require D/S2 and values in `1..physical PP`; chunk caps must
+fit the configured hard budget, with B3 restricted to 256. Commands are opt-in;
+the default remains AUTO. An initial intent is posted before request replay,
+then monitored concurrently with real workload progress. The client handles both
+local statuses and DP envelopes and waits for every engine: pending=false,
+the matching requested/manual intent, and (for m) ACTIVE, no prefill supersession,
+and the requested effective m. A chunk is a ceiling, so native capacity may make
+its effective value smaller. Acknowledgment alone never counts as applied.
+
+`--control-timeout 30` and `--control-poll-interval .1` bound waiting. Patterns
+wait for each effective m before the next entry, with `--control-interval 2`
+seconds between confirmations/next intents; this replaces the old fire-and-forget
+pattern that could coalesce or overwrite commands. Pending m can remain
+superseded during prefill and can fail to become effective on an idle engine;
+it must settle under real work or time out explicitly. `control_events` records
+acknowledgment/effective/failure responses; `control_errors` makes the benchmark
+fail after saving result data and draining submitted requests. The profile window
+includes any control-settlement idle tail, while HTTP throughput duration ends
+at request completion. Compare like-for-like control/window settings.
