+load cases, evaluation, heatmaps, comparisons and overhead measurements. Each
+step has a safe name, a supported tooling command (including `mixed-profile`) and an argument array;
+`{root}` expands only to the selected output directory. No shell expansion is
+performed. Completed-step records and an explicit failure record preserve progress.
+`pipeline.example.jsonc` demonstrates a complete later-only A/B2/D workflow,
+including fresh Dynamo measurements and owned mixed-window grid data; D explicitly
+uses the retained historical S1/S2 lookup profile, not a fabricated conversion.
+
+```bash
+.venv/bin/python -m benchmarks.energy.beam_tools pipeline \
+  --artifact-root ../BEAM-artifact --vllm-bin "$PWD/.venv/bin/vllm" \
+  --manifest benchmarks/energy/pipeline.example.jsonc --output /absolute/new-pipeline
+```
+
+Eight-GPU provisioning, distributed placement and asymmetry are operator/
+orchestrator responsibilities. No original shell workflow is unchanged-runnable
+against the port: obsolete paths/classes/control calls must not be reintroduced.
+
+## Energy domains and evaluation
+
+Target evidence: `vllm/v1/worker/beam_profiler.py:103–135,184–223`.
+`elapsed_time` is deferred CUDA-event **milliseconds** around the actual model
+forward; it excludes sampling and is not wall/PP latency. `batch_size` is padded
+forward tokens; `num_scheduled_tokens` is unpadded work. Host start/current times
+are enqueue timestamps. `curr_energy` is cumulative physical-device **mJ** at
+host enqueue end, not energy consumed by that forward. `stop()` synchronizes
+each queued end event, then takes a **new final counter sample**. Therefore use
+`energy_log_GPU_R.csv` start/stop `total_energy_mj` difference, divided by 1000
+for Joules. Never sum batch `curr_energy`, and never divide that cumulative
+counter by batch tokens to manufacture energy/token.
+
+The summarizer verifies complete rank files, finite monotonic counters, positive
+monotonic window intervals, and timing-domain columns. It reports per-rank
+window Joules/seconds/Watts and forward timing independently. Windows include
+RPC/host idle and other activity on the same GPU; rank boundaries are staggered.
+Unique physical UUID mapping is required before Dynamo sums per-rank powers.
+No aggregate per-forward energy or isolated model energy is claimed. Long runs
+need bounded windows because the existing worker holds O(forwards) events until
+stop; this tooling does not modify that worker implementation.
+
+```bash
+.venv/bin/python -m benchmarks.energy.beam_tools evaluate \
+  --result /absolute/new-e2e-run/result.json \
+  --profile-dir /absolute/new-e2e-run/worker --ranks 4 \
+  --alignment token --svg /absolute/new-ttft.svg --output /absolute/new-evaluation.json
+```
+
+For standalone evaluation/Dynamo export, supply `--rank-devices mapping.json`, e.g.
+`{"0": "GPU-physical-uuid-0", "1": "GPU-physical-uuid-1", ...}` with actual
+actual UUIDs. Manual CSV evaluation cannot discover placement; `run` and offline
+collection record the actual worker UUID mapping automatically.
+
+Fidelity `token` alignment skips the first token estimate and pairs remaining
+TBT estimates with request-local ITLs only when the usage output count equals
+both the recorded token-chunk count and complete snapshot estimate count.
+Verified complete delta-token-ID coverage is also required. Grouped/text-only
+fallback streams do not acquire a false token correspondence from equal lengths.
+Grouped/unknown streams are excluded from token
+pairing and counted explicitly; no token correspondence is invented.
+`direct` is explicit same-index stream-frame comparison; `artifact-shift` retains
+the figure's historical estimates `[1:]`, actual ITLs `[:-1]` convention.
+Pairs never cross request boundaries; failed/missing entries are excluded and
+missing actual pairs counted. MAE, signed bias and MAPE are reported, with null
+metrics when no valid pair exists. `evaluate --svg` is the small TTFT scatter;
+the full `visualize` command additionally generates latency/fidelity and telemetry
+panels. No legacy plotting module is imported on new logs.
+
+### Dynamo profile collection
+
+Run fixed-clock B load cases across explicit clocks, target layout and offered
+token-rate points; evaluate each worker window with the UUID map, then:
+
+```bash
+.venv/bin/python -m benchmarks.energy.beam_tools dynamo-collect \
+  --result /absolute/fixed-clock-case/result.json \
+  --summary /absolute/fixed-clock-evaluation.json \
+  --clock 1230 --tp 1 --pp 4 --tps 1200 --time-limit 60 --power-mode historical \
+  --output /absolute/new-dynamo-row.csv
+```
+
+This writes the selector's legacy columns `clock,tp,pp,tps,gpu_power,ttft_mean,
+tbt_mean,ttft_p99,tbt_p90,ttft_std,tbt_std`. By default, `gpu_power` is the sum of
+rank powers over each rank's **first 30 seconds of sampled counters**, using
+the last real sample at or before the cutoff, matching the historical collector.
+Full cutoff coverage is required. `--power-mode linear` explicitly interpolates
+the counter at the cutoff; `whole-window` explicitly uses final profile-window
+snapshots for data without a monitor. These modes are recorded in sidecars.
+Power is Watts, never Joules/request; latency columns remain milliseconds.
+Use `run --monitor-seconds 30` to guarantee coverage even for a short workload;
+any resulting idle tail remains part of that window and is not model-only energy.
+`tps` is nominal offered input-token rate, not measured request throughput.
+The collector uses dispatch times (falling back to historical entry times for
+old results) and filters completed latency intervals inside the specified cutoff
+(repairing the original inclusion of an ITL ending beyond its cutoff).
+
+`dynamo-sweep` implements the original clock × TP/PP × TPS × input/output-length
+loops, nominal `floor(TPS*100/input_len)/100` request-rate calculation, at least
+five requests, seeded variable lengths, worker-local clock verification/sampling,
+row export and sorted merge. Unsupported clocks/failed requests abort explicitly.
+The source defaults are TPS 400/800/1200/1600/2000/2400, input 1050, output 150,
+60 seconds and range ratio .3; clocks and intended devices are explicit inputs.
+
+```bash
+.venv/bin/python -m benchmarks.energy.beam_tools dynamo-sweep \
+  --artifact-root ../BEAM-artifact --vllm-bin "$PWD/.venv/bin/vllm" \
+  --model Qwen/Qwen2.5-14B --layouts 1:4 --devices /absolute/A/devices.json \
+  --clocks 1260 1800 --tps 400 800 --power-seconds 30 --duration 60 \
+  --output /absolute/new-dynamo-sweep
+.venv/bin/python -m benchmarks.energy.beam_tools dynamo-merge \
+  --inputs /absolute/row1.csv /absolute/row2.csv --output /absolute/new-profile.csv
+```
+
+A single layout accepts a rank→UUID file; multiple layouts require a
+`{"1:4": {"0": "GPU-...", ...}, "2:2": {...}}` map. Duplicate
+clock/TP/PP/TPS keys fail by default, since different length categories are not
+automatically interchangeable. `--duplicates mean` is an explicit aggregation
+choice, recorded with input hashes. The sweep starts a fresh server per case;
+its startup reuse differs from the artifact but startup is outside profiling.
+Mean duplicate aggregation averages row summaries; averaged standard deviations
+and percentiles are not pooled-distribution estimates.
+Collection, sweep, export and merge require positive integer clock/TP/PP/TPS
+(fractional TPS such as 400.5 is rejected). Required power/latency fields are
+strictly positive and finite; standard deviations may be zero but not negative.
+Rows are validated before atomic CSV publication against the native selector's
+numeric contract. An unexecuted test reads a produced row with the actual pure
+`ClockSelector` implementation.
+
+### Analysis, Pareto, timeline and heatmap commands
+
+```bash
+.venv/bin/python -m benchmarks.energy.beam_tools visualize \
+  --run /absolute/A --output /absolute/new-plots
+.venv/bin/python -m benchmarks.energy.beam_tools compare \
+  --runs /absolute/A /absolute/B2 /absolute/D \
+  --objectives energy_j:min p90_itl_ms:min --output /absolute/new-comparison
+.venv/bin/python -m benchmarks.energy.beam_tools heatmap \
+  --profile /absolute/dynamo-profile.csv --tp 1 --pp 4 --output /absolute/new-heatmaps
+```
+
+`compare` requires identical recorded requests/arrivals and no failed requests or
+manual controls. It also validates matching manifest/result cohort identity:
+model, completion/chat endpoint, tokenizer, monitor minimum/interval, energy-
+window/tail policy, control-wait instrumentation and output sampling parameters.
+Mixed cohorts produce case/field diagnostics instead of a frontier. Intended
+baseline-mode and TP/PP differences remain allowed. Older runs without explicit
+cohort metadata are rejected rather than assigned assumed settings. The cohort
+is recorded in both run files and comparison results.
+It emits all case metrics, CSV, strict Pareto nondomination (min/max objectives,
+ties retained), frontier SVG and twelve latency-versus-power/energy panels.
+`--artifact-filters` explicitly applies the original mean-TTFT≤2000ms and
+p90-TBT≤SLO filters; excluded cases/reasons are recorded. Energy is summed only
+across unique actual rank UUIDs and is labeled whole-profile, staggered windows.
+The default Pareto objectives are energy J and p90 stream ITL ms. These figures
+must not compare different workload seeds/arrivals or monitor idle-tail settings.
+
+`visualize` emits target-schema panels for CUDA forward duration versus host
+enqueue time, sampled Joules/Watts/MHz, S1 token ceilings, S2 owner-phase counts,
+KV cache fraction, S1/S2/full-scheduler overhead, connector host wait/load times,
+client TTFT, offered burst/stair phases, request TTFT/TPOT/E2EL/ITL, and aligned
+actual/estimated latency. Missing series are displayed as missing observations,
+not invented values. CUDA durations are **not** drawn as GPU-completion intervals
+anchored at host enqueue timestamps. GPU kernel start/end timeline reconstruction
+requires actual GPU timestamp/correlation traces that the worker CSV does not contain.
+Heatmaps show legacy profile latency ms/energy J or Dynamo Watts/latency ms;
+unmeasured cells stay gray. Mixed TP/PP Dynamo tables need explicit topology selection.
+
+### Standalone algorithm and worker clock overhead
+
+```bash
+.venv/bin/python -m benchmarks.energy.beam_tools overhead \
+  --trials 200 --warmup 10 --pp 4 --output /absolute/new-policy-overhead
+.venv/bin/python -m benchmarks.energy.beam_tools clock-overhead \
+  --base-url http://127.0.0.1:8000 --devices /absolute/devices.json \
+  --clocks 1260 1800 --trials 200 --settle .1 --output /absolute/new-clock-overhead
+```
+
+`overhead` loads only the actual target stdlib `energy_model.py`/`beam_policy.py`
+under an isolated module namespace, avoiding all vLLM package/CUDA/NVML imports.
+It builds a labeled synthetic lookup grid or accepts `--profile`, times actual
+S1/S1-DVFS/S2/TPOT selectors plus a dispatch baseline, records raw ms/decisions and
+mean/min/max/std/p50/p95/p99, and produces SVG comparison curves. It does not
+pretend synthetic energy values are measured calibration.
+The synthetic CSV is retained in the output directory; profile and actual policy
+source hashes are recorded with the measurements.
+
+`clock-overhead` requires an already-owned idle local dev server with the worker
+extension/auth middleware and a matching `OPENAI_API_KEY` in the client environment.
+It sets the true initial state before every trial (rather than the old cached-state
+shortcut), times reset→low, low→high, high→low and adjacent transitions, records
+worker actuation/readback separately from host HTTP/RPC time, and restores clocks
+in finally. It does not initialize NVML in the driver. `monitor --base-url ...
+--seconds 30 --interval .2 --output ...` uses the same authenticated worker-only
+sample interface. Neither command prints or persists the token. The normal `run`
+harness manages its private token internally; standalone commands are for an
+operator-managed server configured with the same extension and middleware.
+
+## ServeGen and retained data
+
+All five request CSVs, nine system CSVs, five Dynamo CSVs, 43 per-client JSONs and
+original images/scripts remain in the separate artifact. They can be referenced
+directly. No regeneration or byte-for-byte reproduction is claimed.
+
